  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "adr-caching-strategy-1",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "ADR: {{CACHE_PATTERN}} caching with {{CACHE_STORE}}",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "width": 180,
      "height": 52,
      "collapsed": false,
      "status": "proposed",
      "date": "",
      "context": "Read-heavy endpoints exceed their latency budget when every request reaches the database, and the database is the hardest tier to scale. Forces: how stale a read may be for each data class, the write frequency of cached data, the cost of a cache miss storm after a deployment, multi-instance consistency and the operational overhead of another stateful component.",
      "decision": "Use {{CACHE_PATTERN}} caching backed by {{CACHE_STORE}} for read-mostly reference and aggregate data, with a default TTL of {{DEFAULT_TTL_SECONDS}} seconds and explicit invalidation on write for data classes where staleness is not acceptable. Cache keys include the schema version so deployments do not serve incompatible payloads. Caching is opt-in per data class and documented in the service's data catalogue.",
      "consequences": "• Database load and p95 latency drop for the covered endpoints; the cache becomes part of the capacity model.\n• Every cached data class has a declared staleness budget; UI and API contracts state it.\n• Cache unavailability must degrade to direct reads, not to failure; the client library implements this fallback.\n• Thundering-herd protection (request coalescing, jittered TTLs) is required for hot keys.\n• Personal data in the cache is subject to the same encryption, residency and erasure rules as the primary store.",
      "alternatives": "1. No caching, scale the database — simplest consistency; expensive and eventually hits a ceiling.\n2. Read-through / write-through cache — transparent to callers; couples every write to cache availability.\n3. CDN / HTTP caching only — excellent for public, anonymous content; useless for personalised responses.\n4. In-process cache per instance — no extra infrastructure; inconsistent across instances and lost on every deploy."
    }
  ],
  "relations": []
}
